The demos produced highly different output shapes (28×14→1×1, 25×27→4×6, 29×26→5×3), while the test was a 27×27 periodic grid. The first 1×1 output was insufficient to establish an output rule. Later examples showed structured compact outputs, but I repeatedly guessed mappings from visual periodicity without reconstructing each demo's complete source-coordinate relationship. The proposed 6×6 intersection sample with a guessed background was incorrect.

Reusable lesson: separate output geometry, source-coordinate mapping, and output content encoding. For every compact output, explicitly identify which input coordinates or regions generate each output cell and reproduce every output cell in every demo before projecting. Do not infer separator crossings, lattice nodes, or block summaries merely because the input looks periodic. After an incorrect compact-grid submission, discard the complete mapping and content model; another demo request needs a concrete pair of candidate mappings and a discriminator tied to a changed test prediction. Keep tool calls focused on actual transcription and simulation rather than repeated reference browsing or placeholder scripts.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545217/attempts/771c27e3136742b79bef1ff131c04e1e/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-stream-evidence-extraction/references/session-c4fc93b2-review.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-stream-review-lessons", "file": "references/noisy-motif-projection.md", "content": "# Sparse motif projection: review lesson

A demo had a diagonal multi-color marker pattern, while the test contained a compact related motif. The response guessed a single local color adjustment without establishing the transformation. Generalizable lesson: derive the full rule from all changed and unchanged demo coordinates, then apply that operation to the entire test motif; do not preserve most input cells merely because they appear unrelated. Where the demo adds diagonal runs, enumerate the rays and their bounds explicitly and distinguish them from row/column continuation or marker replacement. This is a caution about the analysis method, not a confirmed rule for the particular puzzle.
